# How AutoLevelUp Started

I originally used GLM-5.3-Flash as the coordinator in Orca, with Codex and Claude as workers.

But there was a problem with worker heartbeats. Even simple status signals were being processed by GLM, wasting the coordinator's tokens on messages that didn't really need its attention.

So I decided to try putting a local model, Jev, in between. The idea was to have Jev classify incoming worker heartbeats and forward only messages that actually required a decision or response to GLM. GLM would continue acting as the coordinator, giving appropriate instructions to the workers.

I needed a real development project to see whether this setup would actually work.

I happened to have an idea for a mod I'd wanted for a while in *Pathfinder: Wrath of the Righteous*, a game I really enjoy playing. I wanted to create level-up builds for companions and have the game apply them automatically.

That's how **AutoLevelUp** started.

I didn't have any grand plans at first. I just wanted to experiment with the Orca and Jev workflow while making something I could actually use.

My development plan was pretty simple.

First, use AI agents to get the mod working. That would inevitably leave me with a pile of AI slop full of experimental code, workarounds, and unnecessary complexity. Then I'd strip out the useless parts and refactor the rest myself.

I hadn't really learned C# properly, so I thought this would be a good way to start. I could take apart the AI-generated code, figure out why things were written the way they were, and learn C# by fixing and restructuring it myself.

Once I had finished refactoring, I planned to publish the source code on GitHub.

But once I actually started developing the mod, I realized there was far more to investigate and verify than I'd originally expected.

The game handles leveling differently for the main character, story companions, animal companions (pets), and custom mercenaries. On top of that, there were countless combinations of classes, archetypes, feats, and spells to account for. Much more needed to be tested in the actual game than I'd anticipated.

Along the way, I expanded support beyond story companions to include mercenaries and animal companions, and eventually added Gestalt support as well.

Eventually, I released previews 0.8 and 0.9, followed by the official 1.0 release. People started reporting bugs, giving feedback, and I even received a donation, which I hadn't expected at all.

At some point, I discovered that there was an established WotR modding community. Until then, I hadn't even known such a community existed.

I learned that experienced modders followed shared implementation patterns and development practices to avoid conflicts between mods, maintain compatibility, and make collaboration with other developers easier.

That made me curious about how those developers had implemented the same kinds of features I'd asked my AI agents to build. Were there already well-established solutions that the AI had completely missed? Had it created unnecessarily complicated code for problems other modders had solved much more simply?

So I started exploring other developers' GitHub repositories and studying their source code.

My original plan was to learn C# by personally refactoring the pile of AI slop. But before getting to that, I found myself researching how experienced modders actually write their code.

**As of October 9, 2026, AutoLevelUp has reached version 1.0, but I haven't published the source code yet.**

Honestly, I'm too embarrassed to publish it in its current state. Should I wait until I've properly learned C# before making it public? But that could take forever.

So far, I've been comparing other developers' repositories and existing mods, looking at coding conventions, compatibility, and save-game safety. I've consolidated the research into a final document, but I haven't started refactoring the actual mod code or validating the changes against real saves yet.

What began as a small experiment with an AI development workflow and a hobby project for a game I enjoy has somehow turned into a mod with real users, and now I'm studying other developers' code along the way.

I'm still trying to figure out how much cleanup is enough before I feel comfortable publishing the source.

